#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101648} :activity/id #uuid "6192512e-431b-58e1-939b-ea069ab75713" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048816 :topic/id #uuid "c26fdabe-8dc9-5f7c-b26f-36bc185c740e" :topic/math-academy-id 1165} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101946 :tutorial/content "We can use Cartesian coordinates to define vectors in the plane. For example, consider the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fad622207f396145e930cf74f7905104bf12fa394a9f2f4f5d7ce761cc8e36.png)\n\nThe point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3).$ We also have the point $O(0,0)$ that is called the **origin**. \n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ is called the **position vector** of $A.$ A position vector always has its tail at the origin. \n\nNow let's define two special unit vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ where $|\\, \\mathbf{i} \\,| = |\\, \\mathbf{j} \\,| = 1,$ as follows:\n\n- $\\mathbf{i}$ is a horizontal unit vector that points to the right along the positive direction of the $x$-axis, while\n\n- $\\mathbf{j}$ is a vertical unit vector that points upwards along the positive direction of the $y$-axis.\n\nThese vectors are shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb5694945b6f76f14a4e66b854f5daa56f3e607bd5b24c4f361ed844dbf8ff7e.png)\n\nWe have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OX}=2i, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{XA}=\\overset{\\to }{OY}=3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OXA,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XA} \\\\  & =2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $A$ with coordinates $(2,3)$ has a position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $(a_x,a_y),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a} = a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\mathbf{j}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e58c1aa-bfdb-5848-8973-e4f9d1414a99" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2911 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e773cab3-0933-5cf0-b304-06f482fc750a" :step/math-academy-id 15713 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100672 :question/id #uuid "1985c614-4597-521c-bb2d-289fab264708" :question/math-academy-id "e-4289"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7fab292c-d43d-50bb-b785-f1e15122c6c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573322}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Position Vector in Component Form"} :step/id #uuid "e1279df2-0689-5f28-b3fb-c85e814f18ad" :step/math-academy-id 15715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101947 :tutorial/content "The $\\mathbf{i,j}$ notation can sometimes be cumbersome. An alternative is to use **angle bracket** notation. For example, using angle bracket notation, the vector $2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}=\\langle 2,3 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nNote that, using angle bracket notation, the special unit vectors can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i &=1i+0j=⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ j &=0i+1j=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use **column vector** notation, which is similar to angle bracket notation but expresses the vector as a column rather than a row. For example, using column vector notation, the vector $2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ can be expressed as\n\n![](images/24/2419e5c69bef2cc15f00106e4bf83a17ab2277ed5ea43e8b17f904ca6318873f.svg)\n\nUsing column vector notation, the special unit vectors can be expressed as ![](images/f5/f51854ec5bb92b38e5078cfe96046535f8e8efecf1304ea5cc4605dddec09573.svg) and ![](images/ff/ff6013f4f80815a7ca97b5ba8929c2d39b2a4e0165cbb5a8534d8ffafc4377e8.svg)\n\n**Note:** Sometimes, the round bracket notation $(\\ldots)$ is used to denote vectors. However, we will avoid using that notation here, because it also looks like the coordinates of a point. A point and its position vector are two separate objects!" :tutorial/id #uuid "97a4943a-4e61-5eb1-8aed-eec0612edad6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2913 :tutorial/title "Alternatives to i,j Notation"} :step/id #uuid "07d9ece8-5361-50d3-889c-3a9076d01916" :step/math-academy-id 15716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100855 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100674 :question/id #uuid "ad6fec65-5309-58e4-b899-3302188f0e91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4290"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63fc6e25-89d7-56fb-bd4c-a4b5637b7711" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573299}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Position Vector in Angle Bracket Notation"} :step/id #uuid "5dbadd2e-c21b-5903-858b-090a30f8c708" :step/math-academy-id 15717 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100675 :question/id #uuid "ed5a0713-9dec-5f91-ae4f-26dcc25ef174" :question/math-academy-id "e-4291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49e90ac1-2e90-51de-831b-a061b4ee80ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573321}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector in Component Form"} :step/id #uuid "d5dd9006-14cd-59f5-bc73-d5bb1c5d550d" :step/math-academy-id 15724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101948 :tutorial/content "If $A$ and $B$ are points in the Cartesian plane with position vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, then\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the equation above is true, remember that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, given $A(5,13)$ and $B(-2,-35)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=5i+13j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-2i-35j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i-35j)-(5i+13j) \\\\  & =-7i-48j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9efe79b3-c5f4-597e-a50d-9c536befcf6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2917 :tutorial/title "Expressing Vectors as Differences of Position Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "e3d44938-1b01-5567-a6cb-52d2a2341baf" :step/math-academy-id 15725}] :activity/title "Two-Dimensional Vectors Expressed in Component Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101032}]}
